{#
  IP forwarding for the fabric NIC, via the Node Tuning Operator.

  WHY THIS IS NEEDED AT ALL

  OVN-Kubernetes enables IP forwarding per interface - br-ex and ovn-k8s-mpN -
  and leaves net.ipv4.conf.all.forwarding at 0. An interface it does not
  manage inherits conf.default.forwarding, also 0, and refuses to forward.

  That is the entire return path for an advertised pod network: a reply from
  the fabric arrives on the fabric NIC and has to reach ovn-k8s-mpN. With
  forwarding off the kernel drops it in the routing decision, leaving nothing
  on the wire - the reply is visible on the NIC with tcpdump and never
  appears on mp0 - while every control-plane signal stays green. The one
  direct piece of evidence is

    ip route get <pod ip> from {{ clab_fabric_prefix }}.{{ clab_fabric_router_octet }} iif <nic>
    RTNETLINK answers: No route to host

  EHOSTUNREACH, which the kernel substitutes only when forwarding is off on
  the incoming interface. A missing route gives ENETUNREACH, "Network is
  unreachable".

  WHY TUNED AND NOT THE NNCP

  nmstate grew per-device ipv4.forwarding in 2.2.51, but the build shipped
  with the 4.22 NMState operator (2.2.60) rejects the field - "unknown field
  `forwarding`" - and rolls the whole policy back when it does, taking the
  NIC's address with it. Tuned is the operator-supported way to set a sysctl
  on a set of nodes, needs no reboot, and is version-independent.

  WHY THE GLOBAL KNOB AND NOT conf.<nic>.forwarding

  Deliberate, and the opposite of the usual advice. A per-interface sysctl
  has to name the interface, and this NIC is hot-plugged - its name comes
  from PCI enumeration and is not guaranteed stable across nodes or across a
  re-attach. A Tuned profile naming an interface that no longer exists
  applies cleanly and does nothing, which is precisely the silent failure
  this is fixing. net.ipv4.ip_forward names no interface: it is the same knob
  as conf.all.forwarding, and writing it propagates to every interface present.

  IT DOES NOT COVER INTERFACES THAT ARRIVE LATER, which is why
  conf.default.forwarding is set alongside it. This file used to claim the
  global write "sets the default every later one inherits"; it does not touch
  conf.default.forwarding at all, so an interface created afterwards still
  inherits 0. The fabric NIC is hot-plugged, so whether it was covered came
  down to whether it happened to exist when Tuned last applied - true on a node
  whose NIC was attached before the cluster half ever ran, false on one
  attached later. Same profile, same node label, opposite result, and the only
  symptom is that node's pods never answering anything from the fabric.

  Tuned still does not re-apply when an interface appears, so a NIC attached to
  a node whose profile is already current is missed even with the default set.
  roles/setup-udn-bgp/tasks/fabric-forwarding.yml writes both values directly
  for exactly that case - twice per run, because the global knob this profile
  sets is the same one anything else on the node can zero, and a write to it
  propagates to every interface. See the comment at the top of that file.

  THIS PROFILE CANNOT WIN AN ARGUMENT, AND FOR A LONG TIME IT WAS LOSING ONE

  The Node Tuning Operator runs tuned in no_daemon mode: it applies the
  profile once and exits. `tuned-adm verify` inside the tuned pod answers
  "Not supported in no_daemon mode - cannot talk to TuneD daemon via DBus",
  which is the whole story. Nothing re-asserts these values, so the last
  process to write them keeps them forever, and the Profile CR's
  Applied=True is a receipt for one write minutes ago rather than a statement
  about the node now.

  What it was losing to is ovnkube-node under the default
  gatewayConfig.ipForwarding=Restricted, which holds net.ipv4.ip_forward at 0
  and forwards only across br-ex and ovn-k8s-mpN. Since that value is
  conf.all.forwarding and a write to it propagates to every interface, each
  ovnkube-node restart re-zeroed the fabric NIC. Three identically configured
  workers were found disagreeing - 1 on one of them, 0 on the other two -
  purely by restart ordering, with every control-plane signal green.

  That is fixed where it belongs, on the Network operator CR: see
  udn_bgp_set_ip_forwarding and tasks/enable.yml. With ipForwarding=Global,
  OVN-Kubernetes sets ip_forward=1 itself and there is no writer to lose to.
  This profile's net.ipv4.ip_forward=1 is then redundant and kept only so the
  lab still works with udn_bgp_set_ip_forwarding=false; the line that still
  earns its place is conf.default.forwarding, which covers a NIC hot-plugged
  later and which OVN-Kubernetes does not set.

  The cost is that forwarding is on for every interface on these nodes rather
  than the one that needs it. On a node already routing pod traffic through
  br-ex and mp0 that is a small widening, and it is what the node would have
  had if OVN-Kubernetes used the global knob in the first place.

  include=openshift-node is not optional. The Node Tuning Operator applies
  ONE profile per node; a profile that does not inherit the base one replaces
  it, silently dropping OpenShift's own tuning.
#}
apiVersion: tuned.openshift.io/v1
kind: Tuned
metadata:
  name: {{ udn_bgp_tuned_name }}
  namespace: {{ udn_bgp_tuned_namespace }}
spec:
  profile:
    - name: {{ udn_bgp_tuned_name }}
      data: |
        [main]
        summary=Forward between the UDN/BGP fabric NIC and the OVN management port
        include=openshift-node

        [sysctl]
        net.ipv4.ip_forward=1
        # For interfaces that do not exist yet - see the note above. Without
        # this, a NIC hot-plugged after Tuned last applied comes up with
        # forwarding 0 and silently drops every reply from the fabric.
        net.ipv4.conf.default.forwarding=1
{% if udn_bgp_loose_rp_filter | bool %}
        # Loose reverse-path filtering. Off by default: the untagged fabric
        # session is symmetric, so strict filtering passes. Turn it on if
        # traffic arrives on one interface with the route back pointing at
        # another - that is asymmetry, and strict RPF drops it silently.
        #
        # Two cases need it. VRF-Lite or EVPN showing one-way traffic is the
        # obvious one. The other is phase 2 with an external client
        # (--tags clabclient): the request arrives over the fabric and the
        # reply leaves by the node's default gateway, so every node the client
        # talks to needs this. A node that has it and one that does not look
        # identical from the cluster - only that node's pods stop answering.
        #
        # conf.all alone is enough, despite the usual advice to set both: the
        # kernel uses max(conf.all, conf.<iface>) and there is no value above
        # 2, so 2 here is loose everywhere regardless of per-interface values.
        net.ipv4.conf.all.rp_filter=2
{% endif %}
  recommend:
    # Lower number wins. The built-in openshift-node profile is 30, so 20
    # takes precedence while still inheriting it via include= above.
    - priority: 20
      profile: {{ udn_bgp_tuned_name }}
      match:
        - label: node-role.kubernetes.io/worker
